### sha2.c - SHA-256 and SHA-512 implementation
This is a complete SHA-256/SHA-512 implementation. Key details:

- load_bigendian_32/64: Load bytes in BIG-ENDIAN order
- store_bigendian_32/64: Store in BIG-ENDIAN order
- crypto_hashblocks_sha256: Process 64-byte blocks, returns remaining bytes count
- crypto_hashblocks_sha512: Process 128-byte blocks, returns remaining bytes count
- SHA-256 uses 32-bit words, SHA-512 uses 64-bit words
- Both use standard round constants (K values)
- State is stored as big-endian bytes (32 bytes for SHA-256, 64 bytes for SHA-512)
- Byte counter is stored as big-endian u64 at state[32..40] for SHA-256, state[64..72] for SHA-512

Public functions:
- sha256_inc_init(state: &mut [u8; 40]) - Init with IV, zero counter
- sha256_inc_blocks(state: &mut [u8; 40], in: &[u8], inblocks: usize) - Process full blocks
- sha256_inc_finalize(out: &mut [u8; 32], state: &mut [u8; 40], in: &[u8], inlen: usize) - Finalize with padding
- sha256(out: &mut [u8; 32], in: &[u8], inlen: usize)
- sha512_inc_init(state: &mut [u8; 72])
- sha512_inc_blocks(state: &mut [u8; 72], in: &[u8], inblocks: usize)
- sha512_inc_finalize(out: &mut [u8; 64], state: &mut [u8; 72], in: &[u8], inlen: usize)
- sha512(out: &mut [u8; 64], in: &[u8], inlen: usize)
- mgf1_256(out, outlen, in, inlen) - MGF1 using SHA-256
- mgf1_512(out, outlen, in, inlen) - MGF1 using SHA-512
- seed_state(ctx: &mut SpxCtx) - Precompute SHA state with pub_seed

IMPORTANT SHA-256 finalize padding:
```c
padded[inlen] = 0x80;
if (inlen < 56) {
    // pad to 56 bytes, then 8-byte big-endian bit count
    padded[56..64] = bytes >> 53, bytes >> 45, ..., bytes << 3
} else {
    // pad to 120 bytes, then 8-byte big-endian bit count
    padded[120..128] = bytes >> 53, bytes >> 45, ..., bytes << 3
}
```
The bit count is `bytes * 8` stored as big-endian, but the C code stores it as individual byte shifts.

SHA-512 finalize is similar but with 128-byte blocks and different padding positions.

IMPORTANT: The sha256_inc_finalize function processes remaining data:
```c
crypto_hashblocks_sha256(state, in, inlen);  // process full blocks
in += inlen;
inlen &= 63;  // remaining bytes
in -= inlen;   // back up to start of remaining
```
This means it processes as many full 64-byte blocks as possible, then handles the remainder.

### hash_sha2.c
Uses conditional compilation based on SPX_N >= 24 (SPX_SHA512 flag):
- When SPX_SHA512 is true (N >= 24): uses SHA-512 for gen_message_random and hash_message
- When SPX_SHA512 is false (N < 24): uses SHA-256 for everything
